<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The standalone keyboard.

  Everything removed here belongs to parent supervision, and the point of removing it is the
  Play declarations rather than the bytes. A build that declares `isMonitoringTool` falls under
  Play's Stalkerware policy and is reviewed as a monitoring app; a build that requests
  POST_NOTIFICATIONS has to justify it on the data-safety form. The solo flavor has no
  supervision to justify, so it says nothing rather than saying "unused".

  The Kotlin for supervision still compiles into this variant - only its manifest entries are
  gone. Nothing can reach it: `verify_base_url` is blank here, and SetupActivity hides the whole
  supervision card when it is (see `renderSupervision`). Both gates are deliberate. The manifest
  is what review sees; the runtime check is what protects a user.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      INTERNET goes too, and it is the most valuable removal here.

      With no endpoint configured this build already made no requests, but that is a property of
      a string resource - true, and invisible to anyone reviewing the app. Dropping the
      permission makes it a property of the package instead: the OS will not let this build open
      a socket regardless of what its code does, which is what turns "we do not transmit your
      typing" from a promise into something a user can verify from the store listing.

      The networking classes still compile into this variant and are still unreachable behind the
      blank endpoint. If one were ever reached it would throw rather than transmit, which is the
      correct failure for a keyboard that has told its users it has no network.
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.INTERNET"
        tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE"
        tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS"
        tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED"
        tools:node="remove" />

    <!--
      SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW stays.

      It is the one permission this flavor keeps, and the claim it makes has changed shape
      rather than weakened. Solo used to say "this app declares no permissions at all"; it now
      says "this app can draw a warning over other apps, and still cannot open a socket". The
      second is a narrower claim but it is the one that matters, because the thing a user of a
      safety keyboard is actually worried about is where their typing goes, not whether a bar
      can appear on screen.

      The accessibility service is also kept, and its presence is the honest cost of the overlay
      design: a solo build now reads text in other apps where before it only saw what was typed
      on its own keys. Dropping INTERNET is what keeps that defensible - the OS will not let
      this build transmit any of it regardless of what its code does.
    -->


    <application>
        <meta-data
            android:name="isMonitoringTool"
            tools:node="remove" />

        <activity
            android:name=".ParentActivity"
            tools:node="remove" />

        <service
            android:name=".family.SupervisionJobService"
            tools:node="remove" />
    </application>
</manifest>
